window.SHORTS=[{"t":"key","ch":1,"chTitle":"What Is Cryptography?","sec":"1.3","secTitle":"What cryptography protects: the five goals","url":"chapter-01.html#s-1-3","html":"<p>💡 <strong>Key idea:</strong> Encryption alone only gives you confidentiality. A message can be perfectly secret and still be <strong>modified</strong>, <strong>replayed</strong> or <strong>sent by an impostor</strong>. Real systems combine several tools to get all the goals at once.</p>\n"},{"t":"warn","ch":1,"chTitle":"What Is Cryptography?","sec":"1.6","secTitle":"Encoding vs. encryption vs. hashing (a very common mix-up)","url":"chapter-01.html#s-1-6","html":"<p>⚠️ <strong>Watch out:</strong> If someone says a password is &quot;encrypted with Base64,&quot; the password is not protected at all. Base64 is a format, like writing in capital letters.</p>\n"},{"t":"key","ch":1,"chTitle":"What Is Cryptography?","sec":"1.7","secTitle":"Kerckhoffs's principle: the lock design is public, the key is secret","url":"chapter-01.html#s-1-7","html":"<p>💡 <strong>Key idea:</strong> In modern cryptography, <strong>everything is public except the key</strong>. If your security depends on attackers not knowing how your system works, it is not secure.</p>\n"},{"t":"quiz","ch":1,"chTitle":"What Is Cryptography?","url":"chapter-01.html","q":"Encryption on its own gives you which goal?","o":["Confidentiality only","Confidentiality and integrity","All five goals","Non-repudiation"],"a":0,"why":"A perfectly secret message can still be modified, replayed or sent by an impostor. Real systems combine several tools.","id":"q1-0"},{"t":"quiz","ch":1,"chTitle":"What Is Cryptography?","url":"chapter-01.html","q":"What can Mallory do that Eve can’t?","o":["Only listen","Listen, block, modify, replay and inject messages","Issue certificates","Nothing, they are the same"],"a":1,"why":"Eve is a passive eavesdropper. Mallory is an active attacker, and on the internet you should assume she is on the line.","id":"q1-1"},{"t":"quiz","ch":1,"chTitle":"What Is Cryptography?","url":"chapter-01.html","q":"<code>SGVsbG8sIFdvcmxkIQ==</code> is…","o":["AES ciphertext","A SHA-256 hash","Base64 encoding that anyone can decode","A private key"],"a":2,"why":"It is just “Hello, World!” in Base64. Encoding changes the format and gives zero security.","id":"q1-2"},{"t":"quiz","ch":1,"chTitle":"What Is Cryptography?","url":"chapter-01.html","q":"Kerckhoffs’s principle says a cryptosystem should stay secure even if…","o":["the key leaks","everything except the key is public","nobody knows the algorithm","attackers have unlimited time"],"a":1,"why":"The lock design is public; only the key is secret. Security through obscurity eventually fails.","id":"q1-3"},{"t":"key","ch":2,"chTitle":"A Quick Trip Through History","sec":"2.1","secTitle":"The Caesar cipher (about 50 BC)","url":"chapter-02.html#s-2-1","html":"<p>💡 <strong>Lesson 1:</strong> If the number of possible keys is small, the cipher is broken, no matter how clever it looks.</p>\n"},{"t":"key","ch":2,"chTitle":"A Quick Trip Through History","sec":"2.2","secTitle":"Substitution ciphers and frequency analysis (9th century)","url":"chapter-02.html#s-2-2","html":"<p>💡 <strong>Lesson 2:</strong> A huge key space isn&#39;t enough. If the ciphertext leaks <strong>patterns</strong> from the plaintext, attackers will exploit them. Modern ciphers must make ciphertext look completely random.</p>\n"},{"t":"key","ch":2,"chTitle":"A Quick Trip Through History","sec":"2.3","secTitle":"The Vigenère cipher (1500s): \"the indecipherable cipher\"","url":"chapter-02.html#s-2-3","html":"<p>💡 <strong>Lesson 3:</strong> Repetition is the enemy. Any time a key or pattern repeats, an attacker gets a foothold. You&#39;ll see this again with &quot;nonce reuse&quot; in Chapters 4 and 5.</p>\n"},{"t":"key","ch":2,"chTitle":"A Quick Trip Through History","sec":"2.4","secTitle":"The Enigma machine (World War II)","url":"chapter-02.html#s-2-4","html":"<p>💡 <strong>Lesson 4:</strong> Systems usually fail through <strong>design shortcuts and human habits</strong>, not because someone tried every key. This is still true. Chapter 18 is full of modern examples.</p>\n"},{"t":"story","ch":2,"chTitle":"A Quick Trip Through History","sec":"2.5","secTitle":"The one-time pad: perfect secrecy (with a catch)","url":"chapter-02.html#s-2-5","html":"<p>📜 <strong>True story: Venona.</strong> During the 1940s, the Soviet Union, under wartime pressure, printed some one-time pad pages <strong>twice</strong>. US analysts noticed, and over decades decrypted thousands of Soviet messages, exposing spies including those in the atomic bomb project. Reusing a &quot;one-time&quot; pad destroyed its perfect security.</p>"},{"t":"key","ch":2,"chTitle":"A Quick Trip Through History","sec":"2.5","secTitle":"The one-time pad: perfect secrecy (with a catch)","url":"chapter-02.html#s-2-5","html":"<p>💡 <strong>Lesson 5:</strong> &quot;One-time&quot; really means <strong>one time</strong>. Reusing a key that was meant to be used once is one of the most common ways cryptography fails.</p>\n"},{"t":"quiz","ch":2,"chTitle":"A Quick Trip Through History","url":"chapter-02.html","q":"How many useful keys does a Caesar cipher have?","o":["3","25","26!","256"],"a":1,"why":"Only 25 shifts are possible, so an attacker just tries them all: a brute-force attack.","id":"q2-0"},{"t":"quiz","ch":2,"chTitle":"A Quick Trip Through History","url":"chapter-02.html","q":"Al-Kindi broke substitution ciphers using…","o":["brute force","frequency analysis","an early computer","a stolen key"],"a":1,"why":"Letters like E, T, A, O appear more often in English. Patterns in the plaintext leak into the ciphertext.","id":"q2-1"},{"t":"quiz","ch":2,"chTitle":"A Quick Trip Through History","url":"chapter-02.html","q":"Why did the Vigenère cipher eventually fall?","o":["Its keyword repeats","It only used 10 letters","The key was printed in newspapers","It encrypted letters as themselves"],"a":0,"why":"Repetition is the enemy. Find the keyword length and each group is just a Caesar cipher.","id":"q2-2"},{"t":"quiz","ch":2,"chTitle":"A Quick Trip Through History","url":"chapter-02.html","q":"The Venona project decrypted Soviet one-time-pad messages because…","o":["the pads were too short","some pad pages were printed twice","Enigma leaked them","the pads were not random"],"a":1,"why":"“One-time” really means one time. Reusing a pad destroys its perfect secrecy.","id":"q2-3"},{"t":"warn","ch":3,"chTitle":"The Math Toolbox, Gently","sec":"3.3","secTitle":"XOR: the most important operation in cryptography","url":"chapter-03.html#s-3-3","html":"<p>⚠️ <strong>Watch out (the &quot;two-time pad&quot;):</strong> if you XOR two <em>different</em> messages with the <em>same</em> key, an attacker can XOR the two ciphertexts together. The key cancels out (<code>K ⊕ K = 0</code>), leaving <code>M1 ⊕ M2</code>, which is often easy to untangle. That&#39;s the Venona mistake again.</p>\n"},{"t":"story","ch":3,"chTitle":"The Math Toolbox, Gently","sec":"3.6","secTitle":"Prime numbers and the factoring problem","url":"chapter-03.html#s-3-6","html":"<p>📜 <strong>True story:</strong> the largest RSA-style number publicly factored is <strong>RSA-250</strong> (250 digits, 829 bits), cracked in 2020 using about 2,700 years of computer-core time. Real RSA keys use 617 digits or more.</p>"},{"t":"key","ch":3,"chTitle":"The Math Toolbox, Gently","sec":"3.9","secTitle":"How big is \"big\"? Thinking about key sizes","url":"chapter-03.html#s-3-9","html":"<p>💡 <strong>Key idea:</strong> 128-bit security is beyond the reach of brute force with any conventional computer that could ever be built. When 128-bit crypto fails, it&#39;s because of a flaw or a mistake, not because someone tried every key.</p>\n"},{"t":"quiz","ch":3,"chTitle":"The Math Toolbox, Gently","url":"chapter-03.html","q":"One byte is written as how many hex characters?","o":["1","2","4","8"],"a":1,"why":"Each hex symbol stands for 4 bits, so 8 bits = 2 hex characters. A 256-bit value is 64 hex characters.","id":"q3-0"},{"t":"quiz","ch":3,"chTitle":"The Math Toolbox, Gently","url":"chapter-03.html","q":"(M ⊕ K) ⊕ K equals…","o":["K","0","M","M ⊕ K"],"a":2,"why":"XOR-ing with the same key twice gets you back where you started. Encryption and decryption are the same operation.","id":"q3-1"},{"t":"quiz","ch":3,"chTitle":"The Math Toolbox, Gently","url":"chapter-03.html","q":"17 mod 5 = ?","o":["2","3","12","0"],"a":0,"why":"17 = 3 × 5 + 2, so the remainder is 2.","id":"q3-2"},{"t":"quiz","ch":3,"chTitle":"The Math Toolbox, Gently","url":"chapter-03.html","q":"For 128-bit security an elliptic-curve key is ~256 bits. How big must an RSA key be?","o":["256 bits","1024 bits","3072 bits","15360 bits"],"a":2,"why":"RSA keys grow enormously while ECC keys grow gently. That is why the world is moving to elliptic curves.","id":"q3-3"},{"t":"key","ch":4,"chTitle":"Randomness: The Secret Ingredient","sec":"4.1","secTitle":"Why randomness matters so much","url":"chapter-04.html#s-4-1","html":"<p>💡 <strong>Key idea:</strong> A key is only as strong as the randomness used to create it. A 256-bit key generated from a predictable source might really have only 20 bits of security.</p>\n"},{"t":"story","ch":4,"chTitle":"Randomness: The Secret Ingredient","sec":"4.2","secTitle":"Three kinds of \"random\"","url":"chapter-04.html#s-4-2","html":"<p>📜 <strong>True story: lava lamps.</strong> The company Cloudflare has a wall of about 100 lava lamps in its San Francisco office. A camera photographs them, and the unpredictable blobs are mixed into its random number generators as an extra source of entropy. It&#39;s a fun, real example of turning physical chaos into randomness.</p>"},{"t":"warn","ch":4,"chTitle":"Randomness: The Secret Ingredient","sec":"4.3","secTitle":"Where good randomness comes from","url":"chapter-04.html#s-4-3","html":"<p>⚠️ <strong>Watch out:</strong> Python&#39;s <code>random</code> module uses an algorithm called the Mersenne Twister. After observing 624 of its outputs, an attacker can predict every future output. It&#39;s great for games and terrible for passwords or tokens.</p>"},{"t":"story","ch":4,"chTitle":"Randomness: The Secret Ingredient","sec":"4.5","secTitle":"When randomness fails: real disasters","url":"chapter-04.html#s-4-5","html":"<p>📜 <strong>Debian OpenSSL (2006–2008).</strong> A developer removed two lines of code that a code-checking tool flagged as &quot;using uninitialized memory.&quot; Those lines were feeding randomness into OpenSSL. For two years, Debian and Ubuntu systems could generate only about <strong>32,768 different keys</strong> (based on the process ID number). Attackers could pre-compute all of them. SSH keys and certificates worldwide had to be replaced.</p>"},{"t":"story","ch":4,"chTitle":"Randomness: The Secret Ingredient","sec":"4.5","secTitle":"When randomness fails: real disasters","url":"chapter-04.html#s-4-5","html":"<p>📜 <strong>Sony PlayStation 3 (2010).</strong> The ECDSA signature algorithm needs a fresh random number for every signature. Sony used <strong>the same number every time</strong>. Hackers used two signatures to solve for Sony&#39;s private master key, which let anyone sign software as if they were Sony.</p>"},{"t":"story","ch":4,"chTitle":"Randomness: The Secret Ingredient","sec":"4.5","secTitle":"When randomness fails: real disasters","url":"chapter-04.html#s-4-5","html":"<p>📜 <strong>Android Bitcoin wallets (2013).</strong> A bug in Android&#39;s random number generator caused some wallet apps to reuse random values in signatures. Attackers computed the private keys and stole bitcoins.</p>"},{"t":"story","ch":4,"chTitle":"Randomness: The Secret Ingredient","sec":"4.5","secTitle":"When randomness fails: real disasters","url":"chapter-04.html#s-4-5","html":"<p>📜 <strong>&quot;Mining your Ps and Qs&quot; (2012).</strong> Researchers scanned the internet and found that tens of thousands of devices (routers, firewalls) had generated RSA keys right after booting, before they had gathered enough randomness. Many keys shared a prime factor, which let researchers instantly compute their private keys.</p>"},{"t":"story","ch":4,"chTitle":"Randomness: The Secret Ingredient","sec":"4.5","secTitle":"When randomness fails: real disasters","url":"chapter-04.html#s-4-5","html":"<p>📜 <strong>Dual_EC_DRBG (2006–2014).</strong> A random number generator approved as a US standard was later shown to likely contain a backdoor: whoever knew a secret relationship between its constants could predict its output. It was withdrawn in 2014.</p>"},{"t":"key","ch":4,"chTitle":"Randomness: The Secret Ingredient","sec":"4.5","secTitle":"When randomness fails: real disasters","url":"chapter-04.html#s-4-5","html":"<p>💡 <strong>Key idea:</strong> Always use your operating system&#39;s cryptographic random generator or a well-known library that uses it. Never invent your own, and never use a regular &quot;random&quot; function for anything security-related.</p>\n"},{"t":"quiz","ch":4,"chTitle":"Randomness: The Secret Ingredient","url":"chapter-04.html","q":"Which should generate a password-reset token in Python?","o":["random.randint()","the secrets module","time.time()","hash(username)"],"a":1,"why":"Use the OS CSPRNG (secrets, os.urandom). Python’s random module is predictable.","id":"q4-0"},{"t":"quiz","ch":4,"chTitle":"Randomness: The Secret Ingredient","url":"chapter-04.html","q":"After how many outputs can an attacker predict Python’s Mersenne Twister?","o":["2","624","a million","never"],"a":1,"why":"After 624 observed outputs, every future output is predictable. Great for games, terrible for tokens.","id":"q4-1"},{"t":"quiz","ch":4,"chTitle":"Randomness: The Secret Ingredient","url":"chapter-04.html","q":"A nonce must…","o":["always be secret","never repeat with the same key","be a password","be exactly 1 byte"],"a":1,"why":"Nonces are sent openly. Their job is uniqueness: the same input must never produce the same output twice.","id":"q4-2"},{"t":"quiz","ch":4,"chTitle":"Randomness: The Secret Ingredient","url":"chapter-04.html","q":"Hackers recovered Sony’s PS3 signing key because Sony…","o":["used 512-bit RSA","used the same random number in every ECDSA signature","used MD5","printed the key in a manual"],"a":1,"why":"ECDSA needs a fresh random number per signature. Two signatures with the same one reveal the private key.","id":"q4-3"},{"t":"key","ch":5,"chTitle":"Symmetric Encryption: One Shared Key","sec":"5.2","secTitle":"The big problem: how do you share the key?","url":"chapter-05.html#s-5-2","html":"<p>💡 <strong>Key idea:</strong> In practice, we use <strong>public-key cryptography to agree on a key</strong>, then <strong>symmetric encryption to protect the actual data</strong>. Every HTTPS connection works this way. Symmetric crypto does the heavy lifting because it&#39;s roughly a thousand times faster.</p>\n"},{"t":"warn","ch":5,"chTitle":"Symmetric Encryption: One Shared Key","sec":"5.4","secTitle":"Stream ciphers","url":"chapter-05.html#s-5-4","html":"<p>⚠️ <strong>Watch out:</strong> a stream cipher must <strong>never</strong> reuse the same key + nonce combination. Doing so produces the same keystream, which is exactly the two-time pad problem from Chapter 3.</p>"},{"t":"warn","ch":5,"chTitle":"Symmetric Encryption: One Shared Key","sec":"5.6","secTitle":"Modes of operation: encrypting more than 16 bytes","url":"chapter-05.html#s-5-6","html":"<p>⚠️ The nonce must never repeat with the same key.</p>"},{"t":"warn","ch":5,"chTitle":"Symmetric Encryption: One Shared Key","sec":"5.8","secTitle":"AEAD: encryption and integrity in one step","url":"chapter-05.html#s-5-8","html":"<p>⚠️ <strong>Watch out: nonce reuse in AES-GCM is catastrophic.</strong> Reusing a nonce with the same key reveals the XOR of the two messages <strong>and</strong> lets the attacker forge new valid messages. Safe approaches:</p>"},{"t":"warn","ch":5,"chTitle":"Symmetric Encryption: One Shared Key","sec":"5.9","secTitle":"Encrypting a file from the command line","url":"chapter-05.html#s-5-9","html":"<p>⚠️ <strong>Watch out:</strong> <code>openssl enc</code> doesn&#39;t support AEAD modes like GCM, so this file has <strong>no integrity protection</strong>. It&#39;s fine for learning. For real file encryption, use a tool designed for it, such as <a href=\"https://github.com/FiloSottile/age\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>age</code></a> or GnuPG (<code>gpg --symmetric</code>), which authenticate the data.</p>"},{"t":"quiz","ch":5,"chTitle":"Symmetric Encryption: One Shared Key","url":"chapter-05.html","q":"Which mode lets you still see the picture after encryption?","o":["GCM","CTR","ECB","CBC"],"a":2,"why":"ECB encrypts identical blocks identically, so patterns survive. Never use it.","id":"q5-0"},{"t":"quiz","ch":5,"chTitle":"Symmetric Encryption: One Shared Key","url":"chapter-05.html","q":"GCM = CTR mode plus…","o":["an authentication tag","padding","a bigger key","compression"],"a":0,"why":"GCM is an AEAD: CTR for secrecy plus a 16-byte tag that detects any tampering.","id":"q5-1"},{"t":"quiz","ch":5,"chTitle":"Symmetric Encryption: One Shared Key","url":"chapter-05.html","q":"Reusing a nonce with the same AES-GCM key…","o":["is harmless","only slows it down","reveals the XOR of the messages and allows forgeries","makes the key longer"],"a":2,"why":"Nonce reuse in GCM is catastrophic. Use counters, random 96-bit nonces with key rotation, XChaCha20 or AES-GCM-SIV.","id":"q5-2"},{"t":"quiz","ch":5,"chTitle":"Symmetric Encryption: One Shared Key","url":"chapter-05.html","q":"How many rounds does AES use with a 256-bit key?","o":["10","12","14","16"],"a":2,"why":"10 rounds for 128-bit keys, 12 for 192-bit, 14 for 256-bit.","id":"q5-3"},{"t":"story","ch":6,"chTitle":"Hash Functions: Digital Fingerprints","sec":"6.5","secTitle":"The hash family tree","url":"chapter-06.html#s-6-5","html":"<p>📜 <strong>True story: SHAttered (2017).</strong> Researchers from Google and the CWI institute in Amsterdam created two different PDF files with the same SHA-1 hash. It took about 9 quintillion (9 × 10¹⁸) SHA-1 computations, roughly 6,500 years of single-CPU time. By 2020, a stronger &quot;chosen-prefix&quot; collision cost only around $45,000 in rented computing. SHA-1 is dead for security.</p>"},{"t":"story","ch":6,"chTitle":"Hash Functions: Digital Fingerprints","sec":"6.5","secTitle":"The hash family tree","url":"chapter-06.html#s-6-5","html":"<p>📜 <strong>True story: the Flame malware (2012).</strong> A cyber-espionage tool called Flame used an MD5 collision to forge a Microsoft code-signing certificate. Infected computers accepted Flame as a genuine Windows Update. Weak hashes aren&#39;t a theoretical problem.</p>"},{"t":"warn","ch":6,"chTitle":"Hash Functions: Digital Fingerprints","sec":"6.6","secTitle":"How a hash works inside (very briefly)","url":"chapter-06.html#s-6-6","html":"<p>⚠️ This design has a quirk called the <strong>length-extension attack</strong>. Because the output <em>is</em> the final state, someone who knows <code>SHA256(secret + message)</code> can compute <code>SHA256(secret + message + padding + extra)</code> <strong>without knowing the secret</strong>. That&#39;s why &quot;hash the secret together with the message&quot; is not a safe way to authenticate messages. Use HMAC instead (Chapter 7).</p>"},{"t":"warn","ch":6,"chTitle":"Hash Functions: Digital Fingerprints","sec":"6.8","secTitle":"Hands-on with hashes","url":"chapter-06.html#s-6-8","html":"<p>⚠️ <code>echo &quot;hello&quot;</code> adds an invisible newline character, so its hash is different (<code>5891b5b5...</code>). That catches many people out.</p>"},{"t":"warn","ch":6,"chTitle":"Hash Functions: Digital Fingerprints","sec":"6.8","secTitle":"Hands-on with hashes","url":"chapter-06.html#s-6-8","html":"<p>⚠️ <strong>Watch out:</strong> if an attacker hacks the download site, they can replace <strong>both</strong> the file and the checksum. A checksum on the same page only protects against accidental corruption. That&#39;s why projects also <strong>digitally sign</strong> the checksum file (Chapter 11), and you verify the signature with a key you got some other way.</p>"},{"t":"quiz","ch":6,"chTitle":"Hash Functions: Digital Fingerprints","url":"chapter-06.html","q":"How many people do you need for a >50% chance that two share a birthday?","o":["23","183","365","50"],"a":0,"why":"23 people make 253 pairs. That is why collisions appear after about 2^(n/2) tries.","id":"q6-0"},{"t":"quiz","ch":6,"chTitle":"Hash Functions: Digital Fingerprints","url":"chapter-06.html","q":"SHA-256’s collision resistance is about…","o":["2³²","2⁶⁴","2¹²⁸","2²⁵⁶"],"a":2,"why":"The birthday paradox halves the exponent: 256-bit output → 128-bit collision security.","id":"q6-1"},{"t":"quiz","ch":6,"chTitle":"Hash Functions: Digital Fingerprints","url":"chapter-06.html","q":"Which of these is broken for security?","o":["SHA-256","SHA-3","SHA-1","BLAKE3"],"a":2,"why":"SHAttered (2017) produced two PDFs with the same SHA-1 hash. MD5 is broken too.","id":"q6-2"},{"t":"quiz","ch":6,"chTitle":"Hash Functions: Digital Fingerprints","url":"chapter-06.html","q":"To prove item C is in a 4-leaf Merkle tree you share…","o":["all the data","only the root","H4 and H12","nothing"],"a":2,"why":"With H(C), H4 and H12 the verifier can recompute the root without seeing A, B or D.","id":"q6-3"},{"t":"quiz","ch":7,"chTitle":"Message Authentication Codes: Tamper-Proof Seals","url":"chapter-07.html","q":"Why is hash(key + message) a bad MAC?","o":["It is too slow","Length-extension attacks","It needs a public key","It is not deterministic"],"a":1,"why":"With Merkle–Damgård hashes, an attacker can append data and compute a valid hash without the key. Use HMAC.","id":"q7-0"},{"t":"quiz","ch":7,"chTitle":"Message Authentication Codes: Tamper-Proof Seals","url":"chapter-07.html","q":"Comparing tags with a normal == is risky because…","o":["it is slow","it leaks timing, byte by byte","it ignores case","it uses floating point"],"a":1,"why":"An early mismatch returns faster. Use constant-time comparison like hmac.compare_digest.","id":"q7-1"},{"t":"quiz","ch":7,"chTitle":"Message Authentication Codes: Tamper-Proof Seals","url":"chapter-07.html","q":"The correct way to combine a cipher and a MAC is…","o":["MAC-then-Encrypt","Encrypt-and-MAC","Encrypt-then-MAC","Hash-then-Encode"],"a":2,"why":"Encrypt-then-MAC rejects tampered data before decrypting. Better still: use an AEAD.","id":"q7-2"},{"t":"quiz","ch":7,"chTitle":"Message Authentication Codes: Tamper-Proof Seals","url":"chapter-07.html","q":"What can a MAC NOT give you?","o":["Integrity","Authenticity","Non-repudiation","Tamper detection"],"a":2,"why":"Both sides hold the same key, so Bob could have made the tag himself. For public proof you need a signature.","id":"q7-3"},{"t":"story","ch":8,"chTitle":"Passwords and Key Derivation","sec":"8.7","secTitle":"Advice for humans: what makes a strong password","url":"chapter-08.html#s-8-7","html":"<p>📜 <strong>Clever use of hashing: &quot;Have I Been Pwned.&quot;</strong> This service checks whether your password appeared in a breach <strong>without you sending it the password</strong>. Your browser hashes the password with SHA-1 and sends only the <strong>first 5 characters</strong> of the hash. The service returns all leaked hashes starting with those 5 characters (hundreds of them), and your browser checks for a match locally. The service never learns your password or even its full hash. This technique is called <strong>k-anonymity</strong>.</p>"},{"t":"key","ch":8,"chTitle":"Passwords and Key Derivation","sec":"8.8","secTitle":"Key derivation functions (KDFs): one secret, many keys","url":"chapter-08.html#s-8-8","html":"<p>💡 <strong>Key idea: key separation.</strong> Never use the same key for two different purposes (for example, encryption <em>and</em> MAC). Derive separate keys with a KDF. If one use is ever compromised, the others stay safe.</p>\n"},{"t":"quiz","ch":8,"chTitle":"Passwords and Key Derivation","url":"chapter-08.html","q":"Best choice for storing user passwords today?","o":["SHA-256","AES encryption","Argon2id","MD5 with a salt"],"a":2,"why":"Slow, salted, memory-hard password hashing. Argon2id is first choice; scrypt and bcrypt are fine.","id":"q8-0"},{"t":"quiz","ch":8,"chTitle":"Passwords and Key Derivation","url":"chapter-08.html","q":"A salt is…","o":["a secret stored in an HSM","random, unique per user, stored next to the hash","the user’s password hint","an encryption key"],"a":1,"why":"It is not secret. Its job is to make every hash unique so nothing can be precomputed.","id":"q8-1"},{"t":"quiz","ch":8,"chTitle":"Passwords and Key Derivation","url":"chapter-08.html","q":"Four random Diceware words give about how much entropy?","o":["13 bits","52 bits","128 bits","8 bits"],"a":1,"why":"Each word from a 7,776-word list adds ~12.9 bits; 4 × 12.9 ≈ 52.","id":"q8-2"},{"t":"quiz","ch":8,"chTitle":"Passwords and Key Derivation","url":"chapter-08.html","q":"HKDF is designed for…","o":["hashing passwords","expanding an already-strong secret into several keys","encrypting files","signing documents"],"a":1,"why":"Extract, then Expand with different labels. Passwords need slow KDFs like Argon2 instead.","id":"q8-3"},{"t":"key","ch":9,"chTitle":"Public-Key Cryptography: The Two-Key Revolution","sec":"9.2","secTitle":"Two superpowers","url":"chapter-09.html#s-9-2","html":"<p>💡 <strong>Key idea:</strong> The <strong>private key</strong> is the one that does the &quot;special&quot; thing: decrypting, or signing. Guard it with your life. The <strong>public key</strong> can be shared with the whole world.</p>\n"},{"t":"quiz","ch":9,"chTitle":"Public-Key Cryptography: The Two-Key Revolution","url":"chapter-09.html","q":"Toy RSA with p = 61 and q = 53. What is n?","o":["3120","3233","114","2753"],"a":1,"why":"n = p × q = 3233 is public. φ = 60 × 52 = 3120 stays secret.","id":"q9-0"},{"t":"quiz","ch":9,"chTitle":"Public-Key Cryptography: The Two-Key Revolution","url":"chapter-09.html","q":"Who can decrypt a message encrypted with Bob’s public key?","o":["Anyone","Only Bob, with his private key","Alice, who encrypted it","The certificate authority"],"a":1,"why":"Once the padlock snaps shut, even Alice can’t reopen the box.","id":"q9-1"},{"t":"quiz","ch":9,"chTitle":"Public-Key Cryptography: The Two-Key Revolution","url":"chapter-09.html","q":"Why do real systems use hybrid encryption?","o":["RSA cannot decrypt","Public-key crypto is slow and handles tiny data, so it wraps an AES key","AES is insecure","For legal reasons"],"a":1,"why":"Send the heavy safe by ordinary post (AES) and its small key in an envelope only Bob can open (public key).","id":"q9-2"},{"t":"quiz","ch":9,"chTitle":"Public-Key Cryptography: The Two-Key Revolution","url":"chapter-09.html","q":"Which RSA encryption padding should you use?","o":["None (textbook RSA)","PKCS#1 v1.5","OAEP","ECB"],"a":2,"why":"RSA-OAEP. PKCS#1 v1.5 encryption is vulnerable to Bleichenbacher-style attacks (ROBOT).","id":"q9-3"},{"t":"key","ch":10,"chTitle":"Key Exchange: Agreeing on a Secret in Public","sec":"10.5","secTitle":"The catch: man-in-the-middle attacks","url":"chapter-10.html#s-10-5","html":"<p>💡 <strong>Key idea:</strong> Key exchange gives you a <strong>shared secret</strong>. Authentication tells you <strong>who you share it with</strong>. You need both. Unauthenticated encryption is like whispering secrets to a stranger in a dark room.</p>\n"},{"t":"story","ch":10,"chTitle":"Key Exchange: Agreeing on a Secret in Public","sec":"10.6","secTitle":"Ephemeral keys and forward secrecy","url":"chapter-10.html#s-10-6","html":"<p>📜 <strong>Why it matters:</strong> documents leaked in 2013 described intelligence agencies recording vast amounts of encrypted internet traffic to decrypt later. The industry responded by switching to forward secrecy. <strong>TLS 1.3 (2018) requires it</strong>: RSA key transport was removed entirely.</p>"},{"t":"quiz","ch":10,"chTitle":"Key Exchange: Agreeing on a Secret in Public","url":"chapter-10.html","q":"p = 23, g = 5, Alice picks a = 6, Bob picks b = 15. The shared secret is…","o":["8","19","2","23"],"a":2,"why":"A = 8, B = 19. Alice: 19⁶ mod 23 = 2. Bob: 8¹⁵ mod 23 = 2.","id":"q10-0"},{"t":"quiz","ch":10,"chTitle":"Key Exchange: Agreeing on a Secret in Public","url":"chapter-10.html","q":"Unauthenticated Diffie-Hellman is vulnerable to…","o":["a man in the middle","birthday collisions","padding oracles","length extension"],"a":0,"why":"Mallory runs one exchange with Alice and another with Bob. Authentication (signatures, certificates) stops her.","id":"q10-1"},{"t":"quiz","ch":10,"chTitle":"Key Exchange: Agreeing on a Secret in Public","url":"chapter-10.html","q":"Forward secrecy comes from…","o":["longer RSA keys","ephemeral keys deleted after the session","certificates","SHA-512"],"a":1,"why":"If the temporary keys are gone, stealing the long-term key later cannot decrypt old recordings.","id":"q10-2"},{"t":"quiz","ch":10,"chTitle":"Key Exchange: Agreeing on a Secret in Public","url":"chapter-10.html","q":"X25519MLKEM768 combines…","o":["two RSA keys","classical X25519 with post-quantum ML-KEM","AES and SHA-256","Ed25519 and RSA"],"a":1,"why":"A hybrid: an attacker must break both. It is on by default in Chrome, Edge, Firefox and OpenSSL 3.5+.","id":"q10-3"},{"t":"warn","ch":11,"chTitle":"Digital Signatures: Unforgeable Digital Ink","sec":"11.2","secTitle":"How signing and verifying work","url":"chapter-11.html#s-11-2","html":"<p>⚠️ You&#39;ll often hear &quot;a signature is the hash encrypted with the private key.&quot; That&#39;s roughly true for RSA, but <strong>not</strong> for ECDSA or Ed25519, which use different math. The intuition that matters: <strong>only the private key can create the signature, and the public key can check it.</strong></p>"},{"t":"story","ch":11,"chTitle":"Digital Signatures: Unforgeable Digital Ink","sec":"11.5","secTitle":"Signature algorithms","url":"chapter-11.html#s-11-5","html":"<p>📜 <strong>Remember the PlayStation 3?</strong> ECDSA needs a secret random number (called a &quot;nonce,&quot; <code>k</code>) for each signature. Sony used the same <code>k</code> every time. With two signatures made with the same <code>k</code>, simple algebra reveals the private key. <strong>Ed25519 avoids this entirely</strong> by computing <code>k</code> deterministically from the private key and the message, so there&#39;s no random number to get wrong. (A standard called RFC 6979 does the same for ECDSA.)</p>"},{"t":"quiz","ch":11,"chTitle":"Digital Signatures: Unforgeable Digital Ink","url":"chapter-11.html","q":"You sign with your ___ key; others verify with your ___ key.","o":["public / private","private / public","shared / shared","session / master"],"a":1,"why":"Only you can make the seal, but everyone can check it.","id":"q11-0"},{"t":"quiz","ch":11,"chTitle":"Digital Signatures: Unforgeable Digital Ink","url":"chapter-11.html","q":"Toy RSA: Bob signs hash 65 and gets s = 588. Verification computes 588¹⁷ mod 3233 = ?","o":["588","65","2753","17"],"a":1,"why":"It returns the hash 65, which matches, so the signature is valid.","id":"q11-1"},{"t":"quiz","ch":11,"chTitle":"Digital Signatures: Unforgeable Digital Ink","url":"chapter-11.html","q":"Why can’t Ed25519 make the PlayStation 3 mistake?","o":["It uses RSA internally","It derives the per-signature nonce deterministically","Its keys are longer","It skips hashing"],"a":1,"why":"There is no random number to get wrong: k comes from the private key and the message.","id":"q11-2"},{"t":"quiz","ch":11,"chTitle":"Digital Signatures: Unforgeable Digital Ink","url":"chapter-11.html","q":"Which one gives non-repudiation?","o":["SHA-256","HMAC-SHA256","AES-GCM","A digital signature"],"a":3,"why":"Only the private-key holder could have produced it, and anyone can verify it.","id":"q11-3"},{"t":"story","ch":12,"chTitle":"Networking Essentials for Cryptography","sec":"12.7","secTitle":"🌐 HTTP: how browsers talk to websites","url":"chapter-12.html#s-12-7","html":"<p>📜 <strong>True story: Firesheep (2010).</strong> A browser add-on called Firesheep let anyone on a café Wi-Fi network see other people&#39;s unencrypted session cookies for sites like Facebook and Twitter, then log in as them with <strong>one click</strong>. It caused a public outcry, and within a few years major sites switched to HTTPS everywhere, not just on login pages.</p>"},{"t":"quiz","ch":12,"chTitle":"Networking Essentials for Cryptography","url":"chapter-12.html","q":"Which port do web servers use for HTTPS?","o":["80","22","443","53"],"a":2,"why":"Port 443 (TCP, and UDP for HTTP/3). Port 80 is plain, unencrypted HTTP.","id":"q12-0"},{"t":"quiz","ch":12,"chTitle":"Networking Essentials for Cryptography","url":"chapter-12.html","q":"Which gives reliable, ordered delivery with a three-way handshake?","o":["UDP","TCP","DNS","QUIC"],"a":1,"why":"TCP: SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK. UDP is fire-and-forget (used by DNS, WireGuard and QUIC).","id":"q12-1"},{"t":"quiz","ch":12,"chTitle":"Networking Essentials for Cryptography","url":"chapter-12.html","q":"DNS is…","o":["the internet’s phone book: names → IP addresses","an encryption protocol","a type of certificate","a VPN"],"a":0,"why":"Classic DNS is unencrypted and unauthenticated, which is why DNSSEC, DoT and DoH exist.","id":"q12-2"},{"t":"quiz","ch":12,"chTitle":"Networking Essentials for Cryptography","url":"chapter-12.html","q":"With plain HTTPS, what can the café Wi-Fi still see?","o":["Your password","The page path and cookies","The domain name and IP addresses (plus data size and timing)","Nothing at all"],"a":2,"why":"Contents, paths and cookies are hidden, but IPs, the domain (via DNS and SNI) and traffic volume are visible.","id":"q12-3"},{"t":"warn","ch":13,"chTitle":"Certificates and PKI: The Internet's ID Cards","sec":"13.7","secTitle":"Validation levels: DV, OV and EV","url":"chapter-13.html#s-13-7","html":"<p>⚠️ <strong>Watch out: the padlock does NOT mean a site is trustworthy.</strong> It means your connection is encrypted and that you&#39;re connected to the domain shown in the address bar. Phishing sites get free DV certificates too. <code>paypa1-secure-login.example</code> can have a perfectly valid padlock. Always check the <strong>domain name</strong>.</p>\n"},{"t":"story","ch":13,"chTitle":"Certificates and PKI: The Internet's ID Cards","sec":"13.14","secTitle":"When PKI goes wrong","url":"chapter-13.html#s-13-14","html":"<p>📜 <strong>DigiNotar (2011).</strong> Hackers broke into DigiNotar, a Dutch CA, and issued over 500 fraudulent certificates, including one for <code>*.google.com</code>. It was used to intercept the Gmail traffic of an estimated 300,000 people in Iran. It was discovered because Chrome had Google&#39;s keys &quot;pinned&quot; (it knew what Google&#39;s certificates should look like). All browsers removed DigiNotar from their trust stores, and the company went bankrupt within weeks.</p>"},{"t":"story","ch":13,"chTitle":"Certificates and PKI: The Internet's ID Cards","sec":"13.14","secTitle":"When PKI goes wrong","url":"chapter-13.html#s-13-14","html":"<p>📜 <strong>Symantec (2017–2018).</strong> After years of mis-issued certificates and poor oversight, Google and Mozilla gradually distrusted Symantec&#39;s CA business, one of the largest in the world at the time. Millions of websites had to replace their certificates.</p>"},{"t":"story","ch":13,"chTitle":"Certificates and PKI: The Internet's ID Cards","sec":"13.14","secTitle":"When PKI goes wrong","url":"chapter-13.html#s-13-14","html":"<p>📜 <strong>Entrust (2024).</strong> After a long series of compliance failures, Chrome announced it would stop trusting new Entrust TLS certificates issued after late 2024. Other browsers followed.</p>"},{"t":"warn","ch":13,"chTitle":"Certificates and PKI: The Internet's ID Cards","sec":"13.16","secTitle":"🧪 Hands-on lab: build your own PKI","url":"chapter-13.html#s-13-16","html":"<p>⚠️ <strong>Watch out:</strong> whoever holds a trusted root&#39;s private key can impersonate <strong>any website</strong> to your computer. Only do this on a test machine, keep <code>root-ca.key</code> safe, and remove the root when you&#39;re done. For local development, the tool <strong><a href=\"https://github.com/FiloSottile/mkcert\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mkcert</a></strong> automates all of this safely.</p>\n"},{"t":"quiz","ch":13,"chTitle":"Certificates and PKI: The Internet's ID Cards","url":"chapter-13.html","q":"A browser trusts a certificate when its chain leads up to…","o":["any intermediate CA","a root CA in its trust store","the website itself","a DNS server"],"a":1,"why":"Leaf → intermediate → root. The root is pre-installed in your OS or browser.","id":"q13-0"},{"t":"quiz","ch":13,"chTitle":"Certificates and PKI: The Internet's ID Cards","url":"chapter-13.html","q":"The padlock icon means…","o":["the site is trustworthy","the connection is encrypted to the domain shown","it is an EV certificate","no phishing is possible"],"a":1,"why":"Phishing sites get free DV certificates too. Always check the domain name.","id":"q13-1"},{"t":"quiz","ch":13,"chTitle":"Certificates and PKI: The Internet's ID Cards","url":"chapter-13.html","q":"From March 2026, the maximum lifetime of a public TLS certificate is…","o":["5 years","398 days","200 days","47 days"],"a":2,"why":"200 days now, 100 days in 2027 and 47 days in 2029 (planned).","id":"q13-2"},{"t":"quiz","ch":13,"chTitle":"Certificates and PKI: The Internet's ID Cards","url":"chapter-13.html","q":"Certificate Transparency logs are built as…","o":["Merkle trees","blockchains","spreadsheets","encrypted databases"],"a":0,"why":"Append-only hash trees, so anyone can check that a certificate was logged.","id":"q13-3"},{"t":"story","ch":14,"chTitle":"TLS and HTTPS: Putting It All Together","sec":"14.12","secTitle":"HSTS: closing the HTTP loophole","url":"chapter-14.html#s-14-12","html":"<p>📜 <strong>SSL stripping (2009).</strong> Security researcher Moxie Marlinspike released a tool called <code>sslstrip</code>. An attacker on the network intercepts that first HTTP request, talks HTTPS to the bank on the victim&#39;s behalf, and keeps talking <strong>plain HTTP</strong> to the victim. The victim never sees a certificate error, just a missing padlock that most people don&#39;t notice.</p>"},{"t":"warn","ch":14,"chTitle":"TLS and HTTPS: Putting It All Together","sec":"14.15","secTitle":"Hands-on with TLS","url":"chapter-14.html#s-14-15","html":"<p>⚠️ <strong>Watch out:</strong> never &quot;fix&quot; certificate errors in code by turning verification off (<code>verify=False</code> in Python&#39;s <code>requests</code>, <code>curl -k</code>, <code>InsecureSkipVerify</code> in Go, <code>rejectUnauthorized: false</code> in Node.js). That silently removes <strong>all</strong> protection against man-in-the-middle attacks. It&#39;s one of the most common security bugs in real software.</p>"},{"t":"quiz","ch":14,"chTitle":"TLS and HTTPS: Putting It All Together","url":"chapter-14.html","q":"Which handshake message proves the server holds its certificate’s private key?","o":["ClientHello","ServerHello","CertificateVerify","EncryptedExtensions"],"a":2,"why":"A signature over the whole handshake. Mallory can copy the certificate but can’t make this signature.","id":"q14-0"},{"t":"quiz","ch":14,"chTitle":"TLS and HTTPS: Putting It All Together","url":"chapter-14.html","q":"In TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384, what is SHA-384 used for?","o":["Bulk encryption","HKDF and the transcript hash","The certificate signature","Compression"],"a":1,"why":"TLS 1.3 suites name only the AEAD and the hash for key derivation.","id":"q14-1"},{"t":"quiz","ch":14,"chTitle":"TLS and HTTPS: Putting It All Together","url":"chapter-14.html","q":"A full TLS 1.3 handshake takes how many round trips?","o":["0","1","2","3"],"a":1,"why":"One round trip (TLS 1.2 needed two). Resumption with 0-RTT can send data immediately.","id":"q14-2"},{"t":"quiz","ch":14,"chTitle":"TLS and HTTPS: Putting It All Together","url":"chapter-14.html","q":"HSTS protects against…","o":["quantum computers","being downgraded to plain HTTP","expired certificates","weak passwords"],"a":1,"why":"It tells the browser: always use HTTPS for this site, even if someone links to http://.","id":"q14-3"},{"t":"story","ch":15,"chTitle":"Secure Connections: SSH, VPNs, Wi-Fi and DNS","sec":"15.1","secTitle":"SSH: secure remote access","url":"chapter-15.html#s-15-1","html":"<p>📜 <strong>Origin story:</strong> in 1995, Finnish researcher Tatu Ylönen&#39;s university network was hit by a password-sniffing attack. The tools of the time (Telnet, rlogin) sent passwords in plain text. He wrote SSH to replace them, and within a few years it had replaced them almost everywhere.</p>"},{"t":"warn","ch":15,"chTitle":"Secure Connections: SSH, VPNs, Wi-Fi and DNS","sec":"15.3","secTitle":"Wi-Fi security","url":"chapter-15.html#s-15-3","html":"<p>⚠️ <strong>Watch out:</strong> on enterprise Wi-Fi, your device must check the login server&#39;s <strong>certificate</strong>. If it doesn&#39;t, an attacker can set up an &quot;evil twin&quot; access point with the same name and collect people&#39;s passwords.</p>"},{"t":"story","ch":15,"chTitle":"Secure Connections: SSH, VPNs, Wi-Fi and DNS","sec":"15.4","secTitle":"DNS security: DNSSEC, DoT and DoH","url":"chapter-15.html#s-15-4","html":"<p>📜 <strong>The root key ceremony:</strong> the root zone&#39;s signing key is managed in highly scripted public ceremonies, held in secure facilities in the US, where a group of trusted community representatives from around the world each bring part of what&#39;s needed to access the key&#39;s hardware. The ceremonies are filmed and streamed.</p>"},{"t":"quiz","ch":15,"chTitle":"Secure Connections: SSH, VPNs, Wi-Fi and DNS","url":"chapter-15.html","q":"How does SSH usually decide to trust a server the first time?","o":["A certificate authority","Trust on first use (TOFU)","Web of trust","It doesn’t check"],"a":1,"why":"It remembers the host key fingerprint and warns loudly if it ever changes.","id":"q15-0"},{"t":"quiz","ch":15,"chTitle":"Secure Connections: SSH, VPNs, Wi-Fi and DNS","url":"chapter-15.html","q":"WireGuard avoids downgrade attacks because…","o":["it uses RSA-4096","it has no algorithm negotiation","it is closed source","it uses TCP"],"a":1,"why":"Every connection uses the same fixed modern algorithms. Changing them means a new protocol version.","id":"q15-1"},{"t":"quiz","ch":15,"chTitle":"Secure Connections: SSH, VPNs, Wi-Fi and DNS","url":"chapter-15.html","q":"WPA3-Personal’s SAE handshake stops…","o":["evil-twin access points","offline password guessing","all attacks forever","DNS spoofing"],"a":1,"why":"SAE is a PAKE: every guess needs a live exchange with the router, and it adds forward secrecy.","id":"q15-2"},{"t":"quiz","ch":15,"chTitle":"Secure Connections: SSH, VPNs, Wi-Fi and DNS","url":"chapter-15.html","q":"DNSSEC gives DNS answers…","o":["privacy","authenticity (signatures)","compression","speed"],"a":1,"why":"DNSSEC signs answers. Privacy comes from DNS over TLS or DNS over HTTPS.","id":"q15-3"},{"t":"warn","ch":16,"chTitle":"Everyday Security: Email, Messaging, Disks, Logins and Money","sec":"16.4","secTitle":"Logging in: passwords, 2FA codes and passkeys","url":"chapter-16.html#s-16-4","html":"<p>⚠️ TOTP codes can still be <strong>phished</strong>: a fake login page can ask for your code and use it on the real site within seconds.</p>"},{"t":"warn","ch":16,"chTitle":"Everyday Security: Email, Messaging, Disks, Logins and Money","sec":"16.5","secTitle":"Web tokens: JWTs","url":"chapter-16.html#s-16-5","html":"<p>⚠️ <strong>The most important JWT fact: signed is not encrypted.</strong> Anyone can decode the payload. Never put secrets in it:</p>"},{"t":"warn","ch":16,"chTitle":"Everyday Security: Email, Messaging, Disks, Logins and Money","sec":"16.6","secTitle":"Cryptocurrency and blockchains","url":"chapter-16.html#s-16-6","html":"<p>⚠️ <strong>Anyone who has your seed phrase has your money.</strong> No bank, no password reset, no undo. Never type it into a website, never share it with &quot;support staff,&quot; never store it as a photo in the cloud. &quot;Not your keys, not your coins&quot;: if an exchange holds the keys for you, you&#39;re trusting the exchange.</p>\n"},{"t":"story","ch":16,"chTitle":"Everyday Security: Email, Messaging, Disks, Logins and Money","sec":"16.7","secTitle":"Software supply chain: code signing and secure updates","url":"chapter-16.html#s-16-7","html":"<p>📜 <strong>SolarWinds (2020):</strong> attackers broke into a software company&#39;s <strong>build system</strong> and inserted malware into a product update. The update was then <strong>legitimately signed</strong> and installed by thousands of organizations, including government agencies. Lesson: a signature proves <em>who</em> shipped the code, not that the code is safe.</p>"},{"t":"story","ch":16,"chTitle":"Everyday Security: Email, Messaging, Disks, Logins and Money","sec":"16.7","secTitle":"Software supply chain: code signing and secure updates","url":"chapter-16.html#s-16-7","html":"<p>📜 <strong>The xz backdoor (2024):</strong> over about two years, someone using the name &quot;Jia Tan&quot; gained the trust of the maintainer of <code>xz</code>, a compression library used throughout Linux, and slipped in a hidden backdoor that targeted SSH servers. It was discovered by chance, just before reaching major Linux releases, by a Microsoft engineer, Andres Freund, who noticed SSH logins taking about half a second longer than usual. Lesson: supply-chain security is about people and processes as much as cryptography.</p>"},{"t":"quiz","ch":16,"chTitle":"Everyday Security: Email, Messaging, Disks, Logins and Money","url":"chapter-16.html","q":"A TOTP code is an HMAC of…","o":["your password","a shared secret and the current 30-second time step","your phone number","your IP address"],"a":1,"why":"HMAC-SHA1(secret, time ÷ 30), shortened to 6 digits.","id":"q16-0"},{"t":"quiz","ch":16,"chTitle":"Everyday Security: Email, Messaging, Disks, Logins and Money","url":"chapter-16.html","q":"JWT payloads are…","o":["encrypted","signed but readable by anyone","hashed passwords","random noise"],"a":1,"why":"Signed is not encrypted. Never put secrets in a JWT payload (use JWE if you must hide it).","id":"q16-1"},{"t":"quiz","ch":16,"chTitle":"Everyday Security: Email, Messaging, Disks, Logins and Money","url":"chapter-16.html","q":"Why are passkeys phishing-resistant?","o":["They are longer passwords","They are bound to the real site’s domain and the private key never leaves your device","They use SMS","They use CAPTCHAs"],"a":1,"why":"A fake site gets nothing it can use: the signature is tied to the genuine origin.","id":"q16-2"},{"t":"quiz","ch":16,"chTitle":"Everyday Security: Email, Messaging, Disks, Logins and Money","url":"chapter-16.html","q":"A DMARC policy of <code>p=reject</code> tells receivers to…","o":["encrypt the mail","reject mail that fails SPF/DKIM alignment","sign every email","mark everything as spam"],"a":1,"why":"SPF, DKIM and DMARC work together to stop forged senders.","id":"q16-3"},{"t":"key","ch":17,"chTitle":"Advanced and Emerging Cryptography","sec":"17.1","secTitle":"Post-quantum cryptography","url":"chapter-17.html#s-17-1","html":"<p>💡 <strong>Key idea: crypto agility.</strong> The PQ migration shows why systems should be designed so algorithms can be swapped without rebuilding everything. The next migration will come too.</p>\n"},{"t":"quiz","ch":17,"chTitle":"Advanced and Emerging Cryptography","url":"chapter-17.html","q":"Shor’s algorithm on a big quantum computer would break…","o":["AES-256","SHA-256","RSA and elliptic-curve crypto","one-time pads"],"a":2,"why":"Factoring and discrete logs fall. Symmetric crypto only needs bigger keys (Grover).","id":"q17-0"},{"t":"quiz","ch":17,"chTitle":"Advanced and Emerging Cryptography","url":"chapter-17.html","q":"ML-KEM (Kyber) is based on…","o":["factoring","lattice problems","hash functions only","elliptic curves"],"a":1,"why":"Finding a hidden point in a huge, noisy multi-dimensional grid. FIPS 203, August 2024.","id":"q17-1"},{"t":"quiz","ch":17,"chTitle":"Advanced and Emerging Cryptography","url":"chapter-17.html","q":"In a 3-of-5 Shamir scheme, what do 2 shares reveal about the secret?","o":["Two-thirds of it","Its first digits","Nothing at all","Everything"],"a":2,"why":"Infinitely many curves pass through 2 points. Fewer than k shares reveal nothing.","id":"q17-2"},{"t":"quiz","ch":17,"chTitle":"Advanced and Emerging Cryptography","url":"chapter-17.html","q":"“Harvest now, decrypt later” means…","o":["recording encrypted traffic today to decrypt with a future quantum computer","collecting passwords","mining bitcoin","caching TLS sessions"],"a":0,"why":"That is why long-lived secrets need post-quantum key exchange now.","id":"q17-3"},{"t":"story","ch":18,"chTitle":"How Cryptography Breaks: Attacks and Pitfalls","sec":"18.5","secTitle":"Downgrade attacks","url":"chapter-18.html#s-18-5","html":"<p>📜 <strong>FREAK and Logjam (2015).</strong> In the 1990s, US export rules required software sold abroad to use deliberately weak &quot;export-grade&quot; crypto, such as 512-bit RSA and Diffie-Hellman keys. Twenty years later, many servers still supported those options. Attackers could trick browsers and servers into using them, then break the weak keys.</p>"},{"t":"story","ch":18,"chTitle":"How Cryptography Breaks: Attacks and Pitfalls","sec":"18.5","secTitle":"Downgrade attacks","url":"chapter-18.html#s-18-5","html":"<p>📜 <strong>POODLE (2014).</strong> Browsers that failed to connect with TLS would &quot;helpfully&quot; retry with SSL 3.0. Attackers could force that failure, then exploit SSL 3.0&#39;s padding weakness.</p>"},{"t":"quiz","ch":18,"chTitle":"How Cryptography Breaks: Attacks and Pitfalls","url":"chapter-18.html","q":"Heartbleed (2014) was…","o":["a weak RSA key","a missing length check that leaked server memory","an MD5 collision","a quantum attack"],"a":1,"why":"Implementation bugs bypass all the math. Private keys leaked from memory.","id":"q18-0"},{"t":"quiz","ch":18,"chTitle":"How Cryptography Breaks: Attacks and Pitfalls","url":"chapter-18.html","q":"The defense against replay attacks is…","o":["bigger keys","nonces, timestamps or sequence numbers","Base64","a faster hash"],"a":1,"why":"Make every valid message usable only once.","id":"q18-1"},{"t":"quiz","ch":18,"chTitle":"How Cryptography Breaks: Attacks and Pitfalls","url":"chapter-18.html","q":"How do you defeat padding-oracle attacks?","o":["Use AEAD and never reveal why decryption failed","Use ECB","Use longer passwords","Compress first"],"a":0,"why":"Uniform errors plus authenticated encryption leave the attacker nothing to measure.","id":"q18-2"},{"t":"quiz","ch":18,"chTitle":"How Cryptography Breaks: Attacks and Pitfalls","url":"chapter-18.html","q":"Apple’s “goto fail” bug teaches…","o":["use RSA","test the failure paths","avoid Wi-Fi","use MD5"],"a":1,"why":"One duplicated line skipped the certificate signature check entirely.","id":"q18-3"},{"t":"key","ch":19,"chTitle":"Key Management: Guarding the Keys","sec":"19.3","secTitle":"Where keys should live","url":"chapter-19.html#s-19-3","html":"<p>💡 <strong>Key idea: non-exportable keys.</strong> The best hardware never lets the private key out. You send it data, and it sends back a signature or decryption. Even if the computer is completely hacked, the attacker can <em>use</em> the key while they have access, but can never <em>copy</em> it.</p>\n"},{"t":"story","ch":19,"chTitle":"Key Management: Guarding the Keys","sec":"19.5","secTitle":"Secrets in applications","url":"chapter-19.html#s-19-5","html":"<p>📜 <strong>Leaked keys get used within minutes.</strong> Automated bots constantly scan public code repositories for anything that looks like a cloud access key or API token. Developers who accidentally push a key to a public repository have reported unauthorized use within minutes.</p>"},{"t":"quiz","ch":19,"chTitle":"Key Management: Guarding the Keys","url":"chapter-19.html","q":"In envelope encryption, the data key (DEK) is…","o":["stored in plaintext next to the data","encrypted by a key-encryption key (KEK), usually in a KMS","emailed to admins","derived from the file name"],"a":1,"why":"Keys protect keys. The master key never leaves the KMS or HSM.","id":"q19-0"},{"t":"quiz","ch":19,"chTitle":"Key Management: Guarding the Keys","url":"chapter-19.html","q":"The strongest place for a private key is…","o":["the source code","a .env file","non-exportable secure hardware (HSM, TPM, Secure Enclave)","a shared spreadsheet"],"a":2,"why":"Attackers might use the key while they have access, but can never copy it.","id":"q19-1"},{"t":"quiz","ch":19,"chTitle":"Key Management: Guarding the Keys","url":"chapter-19.html","q":"Key separation means…","o":["one key per purpose, derived with a KDF","splitting a key into halves","storing keys in two places","two people per key"],"a":0,"why":"If one use is compromised, the others stay safe.","id":"q19-2"},{"t":"quiz","ch":19,"chTitle":"Key Management: Guarding the Keys","url":"chapter-19.html","q":"The backup trade-off: the better the protection…","o":["the faster it is","the easier it is to lose data forever","the cheaper it is","the less you need backups"],"a":1,"why":"Escrow recovery keys (organizations) or store them offline (individuals).","id":"q19-3"},{"t":"key","ch":20,"chTitle":"Going Deeper: Proofs, Cipher Design and Crypto in Hidden Places","sec":"20.1","secTitle":"What \"secure\" means: security games","url":"chapter-20.html#s-20-1","html":"<p>💡 <strong>Key idea:</strong> &quot;Secure&quot; always means &quot;secure against a specific attacker who can do specific things.&quot; When you read that a scheme is IND-CCA secure, it means: even an attacker who can get anything else decrypted learns nothing from the ciphertext you care about.</p>\n"},{"t":"story","ch":20,"chTitle":"Going Deeper: Proofs, Cipher Design and Crypto in Hidden Places","sec":"20.2","secTitle":"Security proofs, reductions and the random oracle model","url":"chapter-20.html#s-20-2","html":"<p>📜 <strong>True story: a gap in a famous proof.</strong> RSA-OAEP was published with a security proof in 1994. In 2001, Victor Shoup showed that the proof had a gap: it didn&#39;t actually prove what everyone thought. The scheme turned out to be fine (other researchers quickly repaired the proof the same year), but it was a reminder that &quot;provably secure&quot; deserves the same scrutiny as any other claim.</p>"},{"t":"key","ch":20,"chTitle":"Going Deeper: Proofs, Cipher Design and Crypto in Hidden Places","sec":"20.2","secTitle":"Security proofs, reductions and the random oracle model","url":"chapter-20.html#s-20-2","html":"<p>💡 <strong>Key idea:</strong> A security proof turns &quot;trust me&quot; into &quot;trust this well-studied hard problem, in this model, assuming the code matches the paper.&quot; It&#39;s powerful, but it is one ingredient of confidence, not a guarantee.</p>\n"},{"t":"story","ch":20,"chTitle":"Going Deeper: Proofs, Cipher Design and Crypto in Hidden Places","sec":"20.4","secTitle":"How ciphers get broken: the cryptanalyst's toolbox","url":"chapter-20.html#s-20-4","html":"<p>📜 <strong>True story: IBM knew in 1974.</strong> Eli Biham and Adi Shamir published differential cryptanalysis around 1990 and found that DES resisted it surprisingly well. In 1994, IBM&#39;s Don Coppersmith revealed why: IBM had discovered the technique while designing DES in 1974, tuned the S-boxes against it, and agreed with the NSA to keep it secret. The mysterious S-boxes that had fueled backdoor rumors for 20 years were actually protecting DES.</p>"},{"t":"key","ch":20,"chTitle":"Going Deeper: Proofs, Cipher Design and Crypto in Hidden Places","sec":"20.4","secTitle":"How ciphers get broken: the cryptanalyst's toolbox","url":"chapter-20.html#s-20-4","html":"<p>💡 <strong>Key idea:</strong> Ciphers earn trust by surviving years of public cryptanalysis with a comfortable security margin. That&#39;s why you use AES and ChaCha20, and never a cipher that hasn&#39;t had that ordeal.</p>\n"},{"t":"story","ch":20,"chTitle":"Going Deeper: Proofs, Cipher Design and Crypto in Hidden Places","sec":"20.6","secTitle":"Logging in everywhere: Kerberos, SAML, OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect","url":"chapter-20.html#s-20-6","html":"<p>📜 <strong>True story: Kerberoasting (2014).</strong> Any logged-in user can request a service ticket for any service, and that ticket is encrypted with a key derived from the <em>service account&#39;s password</em>. Security researcher Tim Medin showed that attackers can take those tickets offline and brute-force them. Service accounts with weak passwords fall in minutes, which is why they need long random passwords (Chapter 8). And if attackers ever steal the KDC&#39;s own master key (the <code>krbtgt</code> account), they can forge &quot;golden tickets&quot; for anyone.</p>"},{"t":"warn","ch":20,"chTitle":"Going Deeper: Proofs, Cipher Design and Crypto in Hidden Places","sec":"20.6","secTitle":"Logging in everywhere: Kerberos, SAML, OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect","url":"chapter-20.html#s-20-6","html":"<p>⚠️ <strong>Watch out:</strong> XML signatures are notoriously tricky. &quot;Signature wrapping&quot; attacks smuggle an unsigned, forged assertion next to a genuinely signed one and trick sloppy parsers into reading the forgery. And whoever steals the identity provider&#39;s signing key can sign in as anyone, anywhere: the &quot;Golden SAML&quot; technique seen in the 2020 SolarWinds intrusions.</p>"},{"t":"warn","ch":20,"chTitle":"Going Deeper: Proofs, Cipher Design and Crypto in Hidden Places","sec":"20.6","secTitle":"Logging in everywhere: Kerberos, SAML, OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect","url":"chapter-20.html#s-20-6","html":"<p>⚠️ <strong>Watch out:</strong> OAuth on its own does not tell an app <em>who</em> you are. Apps that treated &quot;I received an access token&quot; as &quot;the user logged in&quot; have been tricked with tokens issued to other apps. Use OpenID Connect for login, validate the ID token&#39;s signature, issuer and audience, and use PKCE. The older &quot;implicit flow,&quot; which put tokens directly in browser URLs, is now discouraged by the OAuth security best practices (RFC 9700, 2025).</p>"},{"t":"story","ch":20,"chTitle":"Going Deeper: Proofs, Cipher Design and Crypto in Hidden Places","sec":"20.7","secTitle":"Paying with a card: EMV chips, contactless and tokens","url":"chapter-20.html#s-20-7","html":"<p>📜 <strong>True story: when &quot;unpredictable&quot; wasn&#39;t.</strong> In 2012, researchers at the University of Cambridge found that some ATMs generated the &quot;unpredictable number&quot; with a weak counter-like generator. Anyone who could predict it could get a cloned card to precompute valid cryptograms in advance (a &quot;pre-play&quot; attack) and use them later. It&#39;s Chapter 4&#39;s lesson again: the whole design collapses when randomness fails. The same group had shown in 2010 that a man-in-the-middle device could make some terminals accept <em>any</em> PIN.</p>"},{"t":"story","ch":20,"chTitle":"Going Deeper: Proofs, Cipher Design and Crypto in Hidden Places","sec":"20.8","secTitle":"Trusting the device: secure boot, measured boot and attestation","url":"chapter-20.html#s-20-8","html":"<p>📜 <strong>True story: BlackLotus (2023).</strong> Security firm ESET found the first publicly known bootkit able to bypass UEFI Secure Boot on fully updated Windows 11. It exploited an old, legitimately signed but vulnerable Windows boot component that had been patched, but not yet added to the <code>dbx</code> revocation list, so the firmware still trusted it. Revocation is just as hard for boot code as for certificates (Chapter 13).</p>"},{"t":"warn","ch":20,"chTitle":"Going Deeper: Proofs, Cipher Design and Crypto in Hidden Places","sec":"20.9","secTitle":"Hiding that a message exists: steganography","url":"chapter-20.html#s-20-9","html":"<p>⚠️ <strong>Watch out:</strong> steganography is not a substitute for encryption. <strong>Steganalysis</strong> tools detect the statistical fingerprints that hiding leaves behind, and once the hiding place is found, the message is in plain sight. If you use it, encrypt first, then hide, so even a discovered message is unreadable.</p>"},{"t":"key","ch":20,"chTitle":"Going Deeper: Proofs, Cipher Design and Crypto in Hidden Places","sec":"20.10","secTitle":"The crypto wars: export rules, key escrow and backdoors","url":"chapter-20.html#s-20-10","html":"<p>💡 <strong>Key idea:</strong> Mathematics can&#39;t tell a lawful investigator from a criminal or a hostile government. A key held &quot;only for the good guys&quot; is a key that can be stolen, leaked or abused, which is why most cryptographers oppose backdoors. The policy debate weighs that risk against real investigative needs, and it isn&#39;t over.</p>\n"},{"t":"quiz","ch":20,"chTitle":"Going Deeper: Proofs, Cipher Design and Crypto in Hidden Places","url":"chapter-20.html","q":"In the IND-CPA game, why does every deterministic encryption scheme lose?","o":["Its keys are too short","The attacker asks for an encryption of M0 and compares it with the challenge","It leaks the message length","It has no MAC"],"a":1,"why":"Same message, same ciphertext: the attacker wins every time. That is why real encryption needs a random IV or a unique nonce (and why ECB fails).","id":"q20-0"},{"t":"quiz","ch":20,"chTitle":"Going Deeper: Proofs, Cipher Design and Crypto in Hidden Places","url":"chapter-20.html","q":"In a Feistel network, the round function F…","o":["must be reversible","doesn’t need to be reversible: decryption runs the same rounds with the keys in reverse order","is only used when decrypting","must be a public-key operation"],"a":1,"why":"XOR-ing the same F output twice cancels out, so F can even be a one-way hash. DES is built this way.","id":"q20-1"},{"t":"quiz","ch":20,"chTitle":"Going Deeper: Proofs, Cipher Design and Crypto in Hidden Places","url":"chapter-20.html","q":"What does a Tor middle relay know?","o":["Who you are","Which website you’re visiting","Only the previous relay and the next relay","Your password"],"a":2,"why":"Each relay peels one layer of encryption and learns only the next hop. Only the guard sees you; only the exit sees the website.","id":"q20-2"},{"t":"quiz","ch":20,"chTitle":"Going Deeper: Proofs, Cipher Design and Crypto in Hidden Places","url":"chapter-20.html","q":"On its own, OAuth 2.0 is…","o":["a login protocol","a way to grant an app access to an account (authorization); OpenID Connect adds login","an encryption algorithm","a password hash"],"a":1,"why":"Use OpenID Connect (signed ID tokens) for “Sign in with…”, plus the authorization code flow with PKCE.","id":"q20-3"},{"t":"quiz","ch":20,"chTitle":"Going Deeper: Proofs, Cipher Design and Crypto in Hidden Places","url":"chapter-20.html","q":"Why can’t a skimmer clone a chip card the way it clones a magnetic stripe?","o":["Chips are waterproof","Every payment needs a fresh cryptogram made with a key that never leaves the chip","Chip cards have no card number","Terminals scan fingerprints"],"a":1,"why":"The ARQC is a MAC over the amount, a fresh unpredictable number and a counter. Copied data can’t produce a new one, and old ones can’t be replayed.","id":"q20-4"},{"t":"quiz","ch":21,"chTitle":"Cheat Sheet: What to Use Today","url":"chapter-21.html","q":"You need to encrypt data with a shared key. Use…","o":["AES-ECB","AES-256-GCM or ChaCha20-Poly1305","RC4","Base64"],"a":1,"why":"An AEAD. XChaCha20-Poly1305 if you want random nonces.","id":"q21-0"},{"t":"quiz","ch":21,"chTitle":"Cheat Sheet: What to Use Today","url":"chapter-21.html","q":"You need to store user passwords. Use…","o":["SHA-256","Argon2id","AES","MD5"],"a":1,"why":"scrypt or bcrypt are fine; PBKDF2 with 600,000+ iterations if FIPS is required.","id":"q21-1"},{"t":"quiz","ch":21,"chTitle":"Cheat Sheet: What to Use Today","url":"chapter-21.html","q":"You need to sign data. First choice?","o":["Ed25519","DSA","RSA-1024","HMAC"],"a":0,"why":"Ed25519 or ECDSA P-256. RSA-PSS 3072+ where RSA is required; ML-DSA for post-quantum.","id":"q21-2"},{"t":"quiz","ch":21,"chTitle":"Cheat Sheet: What to Use Today","url":"chapter-21.html","q":"Which belongs on the “avoid” list?","o":["TLS 1.3","X25519","Static RSA key exchange","HKDF"],"a":2,"why":"No forward secrecy. Use ECDHE / X25519 instead.","id":"q21-3"},{"t":"quiz","ch":22,"chTitle":"Glossary","url":"chapter-22.html","q":"AEAD stands for…","o":["Advanced Encryption And Decryption","Authenticated Encryption with Associated Data","Asymmetric Elliptic Algorithm Design","Automatic Encrypted Archive Data"],"a":1,"why":"Encryption that also detects tampering: AES-GCM, ChaCha20-Poly1305.","id":"q22-0"},{"t":"quiz","ch":22,"chTitle":"Glossary","url":"chapter-22.html","q":"A CSPRNG is…","o":["a certificate format","a cryptographically secure pseudo-random number generator","a cipher suite","a key-exchange protocol"],"a":1,"why":"Seeded with true randomness and unpredictable even after lots of output.","id":"q22-1"},{"t":"quiz","ch":22,"chTitle":"Glossary","url":"chapter-22.html","q":"A KEM…","o":["generates a fresh secret and wraps it for someone’s public key","hashes passwords","signs certificates","compresses traffic"],"a":0,"why":"Key Encapsulation Mechanism. ML-KEM is the post-quantum one.","id":"q22-2"},{"t":"quiz","ch":22,"chTitle":"Glossary","url":"chapter-22.html","q":"Forward secrecy means…","o":["keys move forward in time","stealing a long-term key later can’t decrypt past sessions","messages are sent faster","certificates renew automatically"],"a":1,"why":"Ephemeral keys are deleted after each session.","id":"q22-3"}];
